  "model_answer": "Q1. Requirements for Issuing a Valid Notice of a General Meeting (Section 101 & 102, Companies Act, 2013):\n\n1.  **Duration:** A general meeting must be called by giving not less than 21 'clear days' notice. 'Clear days' exclude the day of sending the notice and the day of the meeting.\n2.  **Mode of Service:** Notice can be given in writing (e.g., by post, courier) or through electronic mode (e.g., email, website).\n3.  **Deemed Service:** If sent by post, notice is deemed to be served after 48 hours from posting (Section 20).\n4.  **Content of Notice (Section 102):**\n    *   Place, date, day, and hour of the meeting.\n    *   Statement of the business to be transacted at the meeting.\n    *   For special business, an 'Explanatory Statement' must be annexed, detailing the nature of concern or interest of directors/KMP/relatives in the business.\n\nCircumstances for Shorter Notice and Procedure (Section 101(3)):\nA general meeting may be called by shorter notice than 21 clear days, provided consent is obtained as follows:\n\n1.  **For an Annual General Meeting (AGM):** Consent must be obtained from not less than 95% of the members entitled to vote at the meeting.\n2.  **For any other General Meeting (EGM):**\n    *   **Company having a share capital:** Consent must be obtained from a majority in number of members entitled to vote, who represent not less than 95% of the paid-up share capital of the company giving a right to vote at the meeting.\n    *   **Company not having a share capital:** Consent must be obtained from not less than 95% of the total voting power exercisable at such meeting.\n\n**Procedure:** The consent for shorter notice must be obtained in writing or through electronic mode *before* the meeting is held.\n\nQ2. Distinction between Ordinary Resolution and Special Resolution (Section 114, Companies Act, 2013):\n\n| Feature           | Ordinary Resolution                                   | Special Resolution                                    |\n| :---------------- | :---------------------------------------------------- | :---------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Majority**      | Votes cast in favour exceed votes cast against (>50%). | Votes cast in favour are three times votes cast against (≥75%). |\n| **Notice**        | Ordinary notice is sufficient; no specific mention required. | Notice must specifically state the intention to propose the resolution as a 'Special Resolution'. |\n| **Purpose**       | Used for routine business requiring simple majority.  | Used for significant matters requiring higher approval. |\n\nExamples:\n\n1.  **Ordinary Resolution:**\n    *   Declaration of dividend.\n    *   Appointment of auditors and fixing their remuneration.\n    *   Adoption of financial statements.\n    *   Appointment of directors liable to retire by rotation.\n\n2.  **Special Resolution:**\n    *   Alteration of Memorandum of Association (MOA) or Articles of Association (AOA).\n    *   Change of company name or registered office.\n    *   Reduction of share capital.\n    *   Buy-back of shares.\n    *   Conversion of a public company into a private company or vice-versa.\n\nQ3. Legal Provisions Relating to Quorum for a General Meeting (Section 103, Companies Act, 2013):\n\n1.  **Definition:** Quorum refers to the minimum number of members required to be personally present at a meeting
